<section>
	<h2>A Brief History of the Crusades</h2>
	<p>To date, four crusades have been launched against the demons of the Worldwound, and while all four have had varying
		degrees of success and failure, none have yet driven the armies of Deskari, Lord of the Locust Host, from Golarion.
		A brief history of the war follows. </p>
	<div class="floatswitch">
		<section class="history">
			<h3>First Crusade (4622 AR - 4630 AR)</h3>
			<p>In 4433, an avatar of the Ascendent Aroden, the Last Azlanti, appeared in Sarkoris to warn the divided clansman
				of the threat posed by the demon lord Deskari. His cultists spread his faith throughout their nation as
				god-callers, and had spread corruption so thoroughly amongst the people that Deskari was able to use their faith
				to create the first rift at the Lake of Mists and Veils. Calling the clansman across Sarkoris and Mendev to war,
				his forces cut through the demonic swarm where Aroden slayed the avatar of Deskari. They then drove the demonic
				host before them back into the lake, where they squirmed back through the rift or drowned. He then sealed the
				rift with his holy power and vowed to personally keep Deskari sealed away.</p>
			<p>In 4606, the death of Aroden sent much of Golarion and the cosmos reeling, ushering in the age of lost omens.
				Deskari, no longer hiding from the wrath of the Last Azlanti, and having spent the last centuries rebuilding his
				power, threw open a new rift in reality. This ruptured the boundary between Sarkoris and his abyssal realm, the
				Rasping Rifts, blighting the land and vomiting forth an endless stream of Deskari's demons who descended on the
				unsuspecting Sarkorian clansman. Refugees spilled across the border, particularly into nearby Mendev, but the
				demon host was one of just many problems to emerge following Aroden's death. The neighboring nations did not
				properly grasp the threat until in early 4607 AR, when the lilitu demon Minagho entered the Mendevian town of
				Kenabres disguised as a refugee, where she slaughtered 62 civilians before vanishing in a cloud of smoke in a
				moment known as the Red Morning Massacre.</p>
			<p>The young Queen Galifrey was an untested paladin of Aroden who worried after her people, when she was called by
				the Inheritor to defend her kingdom of Mendev from the locust horde. She took up Iomedae's banner and
				immediately sent for aid, with a few holy knights answering the call, particularly from amongst the knights of
				Ozem in Lastwall, but their numbers were not enough to stem the surging tide. The church of Iomedae did not
				declare the First Crusade until 4622 AR, as the repercussions of Aroden's death significantly delayed the
				church's ability to respond to the growing crisis. By the time the First Crusade reached Mendev, uniting
				Arodenites and newly converted Iomedaeans by the same holy cause, the demons had long since seized control of
				central Sarkoris, and had claimed significant portions of Mendev.</p>
			<p>The First Crusade swelled the defenders ranks, and they were soon joined by powerful allies, such as the silver
				dragon Terendelev, the great phoenix Pyralisia, and Iomedae's new herald, the Hand of the Inheritor. The sudden
				increase in the enemy's strength caught the demonic horde unprepared, causing them to retreat back to the
				Northmounds. With Mendev and southern Sarkoris thus liberated, the crusaders remained in the region to help
				rebuild - an offer Mendev welcomed gratefully, but one that the disparate and proud clans of Sarkoris accepted
				more reluctantly. In any event, for the next few years the demons seemed content to focus their wrath upon
				Sarkoris's Northmounds, battling primarily with the surviving Sarkorian clans desperately attempting to reclaim
				their family lands, while Mendev remained relatively unmolested. </p>
			<p>Kenabres and Nerosyan were fortified into great cities, and the indomitable crusader fortress of Drezen was
				constructed within these contested lands on Mendevian soil. It became a symbol of the crusade's might, purpose,
				audacity, and ambition. But these victories came at the cost of countless lives, and its scars cut across
				generations as the children of crusaders were born infected with an abyssal taint. Twisted and deformed, these
				offspring were persecuted by the frightened, demon scarred populace of Mendev and Sarkoris. To keep them safe,
				their parents fled underground with their children, disappearing into the safety of half remembered legend.</p>
		</section>
		<section class="history">
			<h3>Second Crusade (4638 AR - 4645 AR)</h3>
			<p>Deskari was not defeated, but simply biding his time, allowing his enemies to think his forces as a mindless,
				contained threat. When a second wave of demons erupted from the Worldwound in 4636 AR, the crusaders had settled
				into their new homes in Mendev. They again took up arms against the demons, expecting a short series of fights
				and boasting that this time they would drive the host back to the very edges of the Worldwound itself. But
				their expectations did not come to fruition. This time, the demons pouring from the Worldwound were not only
				more numerous - they were better prepared. Rather than the haphazard, chaotic, self-indulgent mob the crusaders
				previously encountered, the marauding demons were now legions driven by powerful commanders. Under their
				commanders' direction, the demons orchestrated strike forces, teleported behind enemy lines, drove their enemies
				toward their advancing ranks, and then crushed their opponents between them. The armies of the marilith demon
				Aponavicius captured the crusader city of Drezen using such tactics, along with Iomedae's personal war banner,
				the Sword of Valor, forcing the church of Iomedae to finally call for the Second Crusade. </p>
			<p>Even with the influx of troops from the Second Crusade, however, it quickly became apparent the demons were
				going to win. Fortunately for Mendev, the demons aimed the bulk of their devastating attack westward and
				southward. The impending loss prompted the leaders of the Mendevian Crusaders to make a fateful decision to
				pull their support from Sarkoris, allowing the demon army to descend on what remained of that land. Sarkorian
				refugees spilled across the Sellen river and delivered a powerful but inactive relic, the <i>demonscope</i> to
				Saint Clydwell, champion of Aroden, who martyred his life in a fight against demons to activate it and create a
				hope for the crusade. </p>
			<p>His three most loyal knights oversaw the erection of a field of menhir <i>wardstones</i> along the West Sellen
				and Moutray rivers. Then, under the guidance of the Hand of the Inheritor, performed a ritual to channel her
				divine power into these <i>wardstones</i>, with the <i>demonscope</i> in the Kite of Kenabres as the focus, to
				create a holy barrier against the abyssal horde. The price of their actions proved steep. Many crusaders and
				divine warriors, including the great phoenix Pyralisia, died to buy the time needed, with her rebirth
				permanently corrupted by abyssal magics. But, as the <i>wardstones</i> flared to life, these menhirs contained
				the demons within lost Sarkoris and saved tens of thousands from grisly deaths. Yet despite this success, the
				near-total loss of Sarkoris is generally regarded as the final capstone on a disastrous crusade.
			</p>
		</section>
		<section class="history">
			<h3>Third Crusade (4665 AR - 4668 AR)</h3>
			<p>Now contained within Sarkoris by a combination of the <i>wardstones</i>, increased pressure from the Mammoth
				Lords, and the distraction of an entire nation to plunder, the demons continued to press against the borders but
				seemed largely content to revel in their captured realm. Meanwhile, as the years passed, the Mendevian crusaders
				grew more and more corrupt - in part due to the subtle machinations of the cult of Baphomet, which had
				successfully infiltrated numerous companies and faiths throughout Mendev, but also because the resource-strained
				church of Iomedae had increasingly accepted less trustworthy members into its war effort. The church called
				the Third Crusade primarily as an attempt to galvanize the crusaders, but the long shadow of the Red Morning
				Massacre and the swift discovery of cultists in positions of power increasingly turned the focus of the crusade
				towards self-destructive witch hunts and internal squabbling, where overzealous and paranoid inquisitors burned
				the innocent alongside the guilty. It took three long years for the fires of the Third Crusade to burn out
				before it collapsed under its own corrupt weight. Ultimately, the Third Crusade accomplished very little within
				the Worldwound.</p>
		</section>
		<section class="history">
			<h3>Fourth Crusade (4692 AR - 4707 AR)</h3>
			<p>Deskari was not idle during this time. After decades of Abyssal rule, he recruits the mighty balor lord,
				Khorramzadeh the Storm King and ruler of the Sarkorian capital of Iz. His first assault on the border caught the
				crusaders off guard, and resulted in no less catastrophic an event than the cracking of the Kenabres
				<i>wardstone</i> before Terendelev was able to drive him off. In response, the church of Iomedae called for the
				Fourth Crusade, but mercenaries and "low" templars outweigh the true believers, with many feeling the character
				of the crusades had changed. This crusade proved to be the longest and most grueling of the crusades yet,
				lasting 15 years and petering out more as a result of wartime exhaustion than anything else. The demons lost
				very little during this uneasy stalemate, and morale along the Worldwound's borders has reached an all-time low.
				Some maintain with conviction that the crusade is still in full force, with crusader camps and mercenaries
				trickling into Kenabres and Nerosyan ceaselessly, but they do not march into the Worldwound in force, instead
				merely gathering and going on occasional patrol. There is an uneasy quiet now, and a weary and demoralized
				Crusade is barely held together by the ageless, tireless, indefatigable leadership of Queen Galifrey, who
				inherited the throne at the age of fifteen, and whose reign has seen over one hundred years of war.
			</p>
		</section>
	</div>
</section>